---
/**
 * Book I, Ch. XIV, Prop. 2: a grid convolution against a graph convolution.
 * The grid kernel has one weight per relative position; the graph
 * convolution has no positions to give weights to, only degrees.
 */
---
<svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 440 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="iso-t iso-d" style="--fig-min: 0px">
  <title id="iso-t">A kernel that knows left from right, and one that cannot</title>
  <desc id="iso-d">
    On the left, a node on a line receives from its left neighbour with
    weight w minus one, from itself with w zero, and from its right neighbour
    with w plus one: three different learned weights. On the right, a node of
    a graph receives from three neighbours with no order among them; each
    edge's coefficient is fixed by the degrees at its two ends, and one weight
    matrix W is shared by all of them.
  </desc>

  <text x="8" y="23" class="t-lab">a grid kernel</text>
  <text x="232" y="23" class="t-lab">a graph convolution</text>
  <line x1="220" y1="40" x2="220" y2="250" class="wire-soft" />

  {[40, 110, 180].map((cx, i) => (
    <g>
      <circle cx={cx} cy="150" r="16" class={i === 1 ? 'a3' : 'bx-fill'} opacity={i === 1 ? 0.3 : 1} />
      <circle cx={cx} cy="150" r="16" class={i === 1 ? 's3' : 'bx'} stroke-width={i === 1 ? 2 : 1} />
      <text x={cx} y="155" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">{['i−1', 'i', 'i+1'][i]}</text>
    </g>
  ))}
  <path d="M 52 136 Q 75 100 98 136" class="wire" />
  <path d="M 168 136 Q 145 100 122 136" class="wire" />
  <path d="M 100 168 Q 110 196 120 168" class="wire" />
  <text x="58" y="104" class="t-key">w₋₁</text>
  <text x="148" y="104" class="t-key">w₊₁</text>
  <text x="110" y="214" class="t-key" text-anchor="middle">w₀</text>
  <text x="20" y="244" class="t-sm">one weight per position</text>

  <circle cx="330" cy="150" r="16" class="a3" opacity="0.3" />
  <circle cx="330" cy="150" r="16" class="s3" stroke-width="2" />
  <text x="330" y="155" class="t-md" text-anchor="middle">v</text>
  {[[262, 92], [404, 104], [300, 206]].map(([nx, ny]) => (
    <g>
      <line x1={nx} y1={ny} x2="330" y2="150" class="wire" />
      <circle cx={nx} cy={ny} r="13" style="fill: var(--paper-raised)" />
      <circle cx={nx} cy={ny} r="13" class="bx-fill" />
    </g>
  ))}
  <text x="344" y="200" class="t-key">1/√(d̃ᵥ d̃ᵤ)</text>
  <text x="232" y="244" class="t-sm">no order, one W</text>

  <text x="8" y="276" class="t-sm">Permutation invariance is bought by forgetting which</text>
  <text x="8" y="295" class="t-sm">neighbour is which. A grid kernel never had to.</text>
</svg>
